  "state": {
    "case_id": "147",
    "case_summary": "A 30-year-old Asian man presented with a 6-month history of recurrent pain localized to the lateral side of his left knee. On examination, there was slight tenderness and swelling over the lateral joint line without other abnormalities. Plain radiographs were unremarkable, and routine laboratory tests were within normal limits. MRI demonstrated a 1.5 cm intra-articular space-occupying lesion invading the tibial plateau. On T1-weighted images, the lesion was homogeneous and isointense to muscle; on T2-weighted images, it appeared heterogeneous with internal specks and was unremarkable on fat-saturated T2 sequences. Gadolinium-enhanced MRI showed enhancement confined to the peripheral rim of the mass, with no satellite lesions identified.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. angioleiomyoma\nB. A localized nodular\nC. Synovial hemangioma was included in the initial differential because of its intra\nD. Angiomyoma (angioleiomyoma) was a diagnostic consideration given its vascular smooth muscle origin and typical MRI\nE. A giant cell tumor of tendon sheath was entertained because of its nodular presentation but was not supported by the\nF. Synovial sarcoma was considered among malignant entities but the MRI did not exhibit the characteristic triple signal\nG. Chondrosarcoma was included as a rare intra\nH. None\n",
